{% set local_problem %}
<p>Here's what 69,000 acres of lake does to the wood around your roofline:</p>

<p>Fascia boards absorb moisture from humid air constantly. At 75-85% humidity — normal for Guntersville — wood can't fully dry between moisture exposures. It stays damp 8-10 months of the year. Add any overflow from gutters, and you've got wood that's saturated from both sides.</p>

<p>Saturated wood rots. The timeline depends on wood species, paint quality, and exposure — but we see damage on lake homes that's 3-4x more severe than the same age house in Huntsville. Fascia that should last 25 years fails in 12-15 near the water.</p>

<p>The insidious part: fascia can look fine from the ground while rotting from behind. Moisture wicks through the wood from the back, creating soft spots you can't see until you're up there with your hand on it. By the time paint starts peeling or gutters start pulling away, the damage has spread 6-12 inches in each direction from where it started.</p>

<p>Vacation homes get hit worst. A full-time resident notices when something looks wrong — paint bubbling, a gap appearing, a section looking different. A vacation property gets visited monthly or less. Problems develop for months without anyone knowing. We've inspected lake houses where the owner had no idea there was an issue until we found 40+ feet of damaged fascia behind gutters that "looked fine."</p>

<p>The math is harsh: installing new gutters on rotted fascia is throwing money away. The hangers won't hold properly. The gutters will sag, pull away, or fail entirely within a few years. Any legitimate gutter job on a lake home starts with checking what's behind the existing system.</p>
{% endset %}

{% set service_explanation %}
<p>We start with actual inspection. Not from the ground — we get up on ladders and check the wood. Push on it. Look for soft spots. Pull back gutter sections to see what's behind them. For vacation homes, we photograph everything so you see what we see.</p>

<p>If we find damage, we measure the full extent. A visible 6-inch bad spot usually means 12-18 inches of compromised wood that looks okay from outside but fails the push test. Rot spreads — we quote for all of it, not just what's visible.</p>

<p>The repair process: Remove existing gutters. Cut out all damaged fascia back to solid wood. Inspect underlying structure for spread to rafter tails. Install new fascia board — primed finger-joint pine for standard applications, composite or PVC for high-humidity areas (costs about 30% more, won't rot ever). Prime and paint to match. Install gutters on solid wood.</p>

<p>For <a href="/recent-projects/">Guntersville lake properties</a>, we often recommend composite or PVC fascia. Yes, it costs more. But if you're already paying labor to replace rotted fascia once, putting up material that can rot again seems like poor planning. Composite handles lake humidity without degrading. Install it once, never think about it again.</p>

<p>What we don't do: caulk over soft spots, paint over rot, or install gutters on questionable wood and wish you luck. If the fascia's bad, we tell you. What you do with that information is your choice, but we won't pretend the problem doesn't exist.</p>
{% endset %}

{% set why_us %}
<p>We've been repairing fascia on Lake Guntersville properties for over 20 years. <a href="/recent-projects/">Buck Island</a>, <a href="/recent-projects/">North Lake Drive</a>, <a href="/recent-projects/">Point of Pines</a>, <a href="/recent-projects/">Wyeth Drive</a>, <a href="/recent-projects/">downtown</a> — we know what the humidity does to wood and which materials survive it.</p>

